{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"01","chapter_title":"AI Conversations as a Teacher","title":"Teacher Persona Setup","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting teacher persona setup to privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher","cat100_supplement_teacher_persona_setup","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/03/08"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_4_1_teacher_persona_setup_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_4_1_teacher_persona_setup_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_4_1_teacher_persona_setup_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_4_1_teacher_persona_setup_calibration"]},"content":"# Teacher Persona Setup\n\n![Teacher Persona Setup textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/04-01-teacher-persona-setup.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA persona is the single most reusable thing you build when you work with an AI tool. Most students type a fresh request every time and get a different voice, a different reading level, and a different set of guesses with each reply. A saved persona fixes that: it is a short block of text you paste at the start of a chat that pins down who the AI is acting as, who the output is for, what facts it may rely on, and what it must refuse to do. In this section you learn to write that block as a real file you can version and reuse, not as a sentence you retype.\n\nThe payoff is consistency you can defend. When the persona is explicit, two different chats produce comparable output, a reviewer can read your persona and predict the AI's behavior, and you can point to the exact line that caused a good or bad result. That is the difference between prompting by luck and prompting by design.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA peer leader asks an AI for help explaining long division to a third grader and types only \"you are a teacher, help me.\" The first reply uses words like \"dividend\" and \"quotient.\" The second reply, asked an hour later, switches to cartoon analogies. Same request, two voices, because nothing told the AI who the reader was or what vocabulary was allowed.\n\nThe fix is not a longer request. It is a saved persona that names the reader (a third grader's parent), caps the vocabulary, and states a refusal (do not invent the child's grade). Once that block exists in a file, every chat starts from the same fixed contract, and the inconsistency disappears.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA persona is a system prompt: text that defines the assistant's standing instructions before any task is asked. A complete persona controls four fields, and each field closes off a specific way the output can drift.\n\n| Field | Question it answers | What goes wrong if it is blank |\n|-------|--------------------|-------------------------------|\n| ROLE | grade level plus subject the AI is teaching | the AI picks a default reading level and tone |\n| AUDIENCE | who reads the output | the AI guesses at vocabulary and formality |\n| KNOWLEDGE BOUND | which facts the AI may treat as true | the AI fills gaps with plausible-sounding invention |\n| HARD STOP | what the AI must never do | the AI offers advice outside its competence (medical, legal, grades) |\n\nThe KNOWLEDGE BOUND and HARD STOP fields are what separate a real persona from a friendly greeting. ROLE and AUDIENCE shape style; KNOWLEDGE BOUND and HARD STOP shape safety and accuracy. A persona that only sets ROLE (\"you are a kind teacher\") feels good but still hallucinates, because nothing limits what the AI may assert or refuse.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A persona line reads: 'You are an enthusiastic, caring teacher who loves helping kids.' Which field does this line actually fill?\" explanation=\"The line sets a mood (tone), which is the ROLE/style layer at best. It says nothing about which facts the AI may treat as true (KNOWLEDGE BOUND) or what it must refuse (HARD STOP), so the safety and accuracy fields are still blank and the AI can still invent facts in a cheerful voice.\" options='[{\"text\":\"ROLE and AUDIENCE only (style); KNOWLEDGE BOUND and HARD STOP are still empty\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"HARD STOP, because it tells the AI to be caring\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"KNOWLEDGE BOUND, because enthusiasm implies accuracy\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"All four fields, because it is a complete persona\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nSave the persona as a plain text file (for example `persona.txt`) for two reasons. First, plain text pastes cleanly into any tool without hidden formatting. Second, a file can be revised and compared: you can keep `persona_v1.txt` and `persona_v2.txt` and show exactly which line you changed and why.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA teacher persona is a concrete text artifact: a saved system prompt that tells the AI who it is acting as, what it knows, and what it must not do. A freshman writes it in a plain text file (for example `persona.txt`) so it can be reused and revised. Follow the steps and watch the persona move from vague to inspectable.\n\nStep 1. Write the weak first draft (this is what most students save first):\n\n```text\nYou are a helpful 3rd grade teacher. Help me with my lesson.\n```\n\nStep 2. Run the four-field persona checklist and mark what is missing:\n\n| Field | Required value | In the draft above? |\n|-------|----------------|---------------------|\n| ROLE | grade + subject | partial (grade only) |\n| AUDIENCE | who the output is for | missing |\n| KNOWLEDGE BOUND | what facts it may assume | missing |\n| HARD STOP | what it must never do | missing |\n\nThree of four fields are blank, so the AI will guess and the output will drift.\n\nStep 3. Rewrite the persona so every field has a value:\n\n```text\nROLE: You are a 3rd grade math teacher in a U.S. public school.\nAUDIENCE: You write notes a parent with no math background can read.\nKNOWLEDGE BOUND: Use only the Grade 3 standards I paste below. If a topic is outside them, say so.\nHARD STOP: Never invent a student's grade, never give medical or legal advice.\n```\n\nStep 4. Record the one rejected suggestion. The AI offered to add \"act friendly and use emojis.\" You rejected it because the AUDIENCE field already fixes tone for parents, and emojis would clash with that. That accept/reject line is the evidence trace: a reader can now see the four filled fields and the one boundary you held.\n\nWork through the same upgrade one decision at a time, predicting each move before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Upgrade the weak persona, field by field\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The draft is: \\\"You are a helpful 3rd grade teacher. Help me with my lesson.\\\" Which field is the FIRST to add, given the Opening Case failure (one reply said \\\"quotient\\\", another used cartoons)?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"AUDIENCE: name who reads the output, so vocabulary stops drifting\",\"A friendlier greeting\",\"More emojis\",\"A longer task description\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"AUDIENCE first. The drift between \\\"quotient\\\" and cartoons was a reader-vocabulary problem, so AUDIENCE: \\\"You write notes a parent with no math background can read\\\" pins the reading level.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Next you add KNOWLEDGE BOUND. Which version actually constrains the AI?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"\\\"Be accurate and helpful.\\\"\",\"\\\"Use only the Grade 3 standards I paste below. If a topic is outside them, say so.\\\"\",\"\\\"You know all of math.\\\"\",\"\\\"Try your best.\\\"\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The version naming a real source (the pasted Grade 3 standards) is the only one that limits what the AI may assert. \\\"Be accurate\\\" is decoration, not control.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Finally add HARD STOP: \\\"Never invent a student grade; never give medical or legal advice.\\\" Now all four fields have concrete values and two of them actively prevent a known failure, so the persona is ready to save as persona.txt.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nTest a persona by what it forbids, not by how warm it sounds. A persona is ready when each of the four fields has a concrete value and at least one field actively prevents a known failure.\n\nRun these three checks before you save:\n\n1. **Bound check:** Does KNOWLEDGE BOUND name a real source (pasted standards, a specific document) rather than \"be accurate\"?\n2. **Refusal check:** Does HARD STOP name something the AI could plausibly do but should not, so the line actually constrains behavior?\n3. **Reuse check:** Could a classmate paste this persona into their own chat and get output in the same voice for the same reader?\n\nIf any field reads as a generic compliment (\"be helpful,\" \"be clear\"), it is decoration, not control, and the AI will still drift.\n\nMatch each persona field to the specific drift it closes off:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"ROLE\",\"definition\":\"Without it, the AI picks a default reading level and tone\"},{\"term\":\"AUDIENCE\",\"definition\":\"Without it, the AI guesses at vocabulary and formality\"},{\"term\":\"KNOWLEDGE BOUND\",\"definition\":\"Without it, the AI fills gaps with plausible-sounding invention\"},{\"term\":\"HARD STOP\",\"definition\":\"Without it, the AI offers advice outside its competence (medical, legal, grades)\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common mistake is treating a persona as a personality. Students write \"you are an enthusiastic, caring teacher\" and stop there. That sets a mood but leaves KNOWLEDGE BOUND and HARD STOP empty, so the AI still invents facts in a cheerful voice.\n\nA second mistake is burying the persona inside the task (\"write me a parent email, and by the way act like a teacher\"). When the persona is mixed into the request, you cannot reuse it and you cannot tell whether a bad reply came from the persona or the task.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk through reusing one persona across two different tasks. You have saved the four-field math persona above. Today you need a note about a missed homework assignment; tomorrow you need a note about a strong quiz score. You paste the same persona block first, then the task underneath, in both chats.\n\nBecause the AUDIENCE field is fixed (\"a parent with no math background\"), both notes come back at the same reading level. Because the HARD STOP field forbids inventing a grade, neither note claims a score you did not provide. The persona did the work of holding voice and safety steady while only the task changed. That is the test of a good persona: the file stays put and the task slots in on top.\n\nIf the second note had drifted into jargon, you would not rewrite the request. You would open `persona.txt`, find the AUDIENCE line, and tighten it (for example, \"use no math term above a 5th grade level\"), then save a new version. The fix lives in the persona, which is why keeping it as a separate file matters.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **claim** on the left, **evidence** on the right. For this section the claim is \"this persona controls the AI's behavior,\" and the evidence is the specific line that does the controlling. A strong reader can point to the AUDIENCE line and say \"that is why the output stays at parent reading level,\" not just assert that the persona is good.\n\nA strong reader of Teacher Persona Setup can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which of the four fields is doing the most work for this particular task?\n2. Which field, if deleted, would let the AI invent or drift first?\n3. What known failure does the HARD STOP line actually prevent?\n4. What would you change in the persona, not the request, if the reader changed?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when a reader can predict the AI's behavior from the persona alone, before seeing any output. Save two versions so the revision is visible: `persona_v1.txt` with the blank fields filled and `persona_v2.txt` after one tightening, plus a one-line note on what changed and why.\n\nA useful final note for the artifact would read: \"I revised the AUDIENCE line from 'parents' to 'a parent with no math background' because v1 still produced the word 'quotient.' I rejected the AI's offer to add emojis because the parent-note context makes them read as unserious.\" That note ties a specific line change to a specific observed failure, which is what makes the persona defensible rather than merely complete.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same persona to a stricter setting: a school that bans pasting any student record into an AI tool. The ROLE and AUDIENCE fields can stay, but the KNOWLEDGE BOUND field can no longer say \"use the standards I paste below\" if those standards are tied to a named student. You rewrite the bound to rely only on the public, de-identified grade-level standards, and you add a HARD STOP line forbidding any student name or ID. The persona's shape survives; two fields change to honor the new privacy rule. If you understand the persona deeply, you adapt those two lines instead of starting over.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how the persona skill sits inside the chapter's larger ideas:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher\", \"cat100_supplement_teacher_persona_setup\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/04-01-teacher-persona-setup-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"04.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries for CAT 100 04.01. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_teacher_persona_setup\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your persona file has a concrete value in all four fields, a HARD STOP that prevents a real failure, and a one-line note tying your revision to the output it fixed.\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_1_teacher_persona_setup_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_teacher_persona_setup","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_teacher_persona_setup","cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher"],"stem":"A saved persona file reads only: 'You are a helpful 3rd grade teacher.' Which field of the four-field persona checklist is already filled?","options":["HARD STOP","ROLE (partly: grade is given, subject is not)","AUDIENCE","KNOWLEDGE BOUND"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"It names a grade so ROLE is partly filled, but AUDIENCE, KNOWLEDGE BOUND, and HARD STOP are all blank.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_1_persona_is_just_role_line","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_1_teacher_persona_setup_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_teacher_persona_setup","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_teacher_persona_setup"],"stem":"Which line belongs in the HARD STOP field of a teacher persona?","options":["Explain place value using base-ten blocks.","Never invent a student's name, grade, or medical history.","You are a 3rd grade math teacher.","Write so a parent with no math background can read it."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"HARD STOP names a forbidden action. The other lines are KNOWLEDGE/task, ROLE, and AUDIENCE.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_1_no_hard_stop","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_1_teacher_persona_setup_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_teacher_persona_setup"],"stem":"You want to reuse one persona file across three different homework chats. What should you NOT put inside the saved persona?","options":["The ROLE line naming grade and subject.","The specific question 'Grade this student's fraction worksheet for me.'","The AUDIENCE line describing who reads the output.","The KNOWLEDGE BOUND line listing which standards to use."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A specific task belongs in your first message, not the reusable persona. Keeping it out lets the persona stay constant across chats.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_1_persona_vs_first_message","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_1_teacher_persona_setup_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision","cat100_supplement_teacher_persona_setup"],"stem":"After filling all four fields, the AI suggests adding 'act friendly and use emojis.' Your AUDIENCE field already says output is for parents. What is the strongest evidence-based move?","options":["Accept it; more suggestions always improve the persona.","Reject it and log the reason: emojis clash with the parent AUDIENCE the persona already fixes.","Delete the AUDIENCE field so the emoji line fits.","Add the emoji line to the HARD STOP field."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Rejecting against an existing field and recording why is the evidence trace; you judge the suggestion against your own constraint.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_1_no_hard_stop","difficulty":"medium"}]}}